{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"75ca9242-4592-40fe-8b2c-1d1742366acd","step_index":16,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"run_command","duration_seconds":0.299788,"tool_info":{"name":"run_command","parameters":{"CommandLine":"cat 01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/AOS/Part-2-Parallel-Systems/L04b-Synchronization.md | grep -n -A 20 -i 'MCS lock' | head -n 30"},"output":"26:- Compare the design, space complexity, and network traffic characteristics of advanced lock algorithms like ticket locks, Anderson locks, and MCS locks.\r\n27-- Evaluate the impact of cache coherence and network contention on synchronization performance.\r\n28-\r\n29-## Motivation and the problem\r\n30-\r\n31-Parallel computation on shared-memory multiprocessors requires coordinating threads to ensure data consistency.\r\n32-Threads communicate by sharing data structures in memory.\r\n33-When multiple threads attempt to read and write to the same shared structures simultaneously, race conditions can occur, leading to incorrect program behavior.\r\n34-Synchronization constructs are needed to prevent overlapping conflicting operations.\r\n35-Busy-wait synchronization is fundamental when scheduling overhead exceeds the expected wait time or when scheduler-based blocking is impossible, such as inside an operating system kernel.\r\n36-However, typical implementations of busy-waiting can produce large amounts of memory and interconnection network contention.\r\n37-This contention creates performance bottlenecks that become significantly more pronounced as the number of processors increases.\r\n38-\r\n39-\r\n40-## Core concepts\r\n41-\r\n42-### Lock and barrier primitives\r\n43-\r\n44-\u003c!-- coverage: L04b-01 --\u003e\r\n45-\u003e [!note] Definition\r\n46-\u003e A lock provides mutual exclusion to ensure that only one thread can access a shared data structure at a time, while a barrier ensures that no thread advances beyond a specific computation phase until all participating threads have arrived at that point.\r\n--\r\n156:\u003e The MCS lock is a list-based queuing lock that guarantees FIFO ordering, spins only on locally-accessible variables, and requires memory proportional only to the number of currently waiting threads.\r\n157-\r\n158-Each thread allocates a queue node record containing a next pointer and a locked flag in its own local memory.\r\n159-To acquire the lock, a thread uses a fetch-and-store operation to append its node to the tail of the shared lock queue.\r\n160-If the queue was not empty, the thread links its node to the previous tail node and spins on its own local locked flag.\r\n161-To release the lock, a thread modifies the locked flag of its successor's node, signaling it to proceed.\r\n162:The MCS lock achieves optimal O(1) network transactions per lock acquisition regardless of whether the machine has coherent caches or distributed shared memory.\r\n163-It provides the scalability of the Anderson lock but with a much smaller, dynamic memory footprint.\r\n"}}}
